From abc37c419151fd8398eef8cabb84e144796a1d79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude-checkpoint <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 04:06:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] bytecode: one branch an instruction in the jump analysis
 on riscv64

The scalar loop asks three things of every instruction of the code,
each with a branch: is it a JUMPDEST, is it a PUSH, is the code over.
On riscv64 that is five or six instructions of the machine, three of
them branches, and a zkVM proves a comparison for every branch. The
analysis of a block's codes is then the stretch of a guest's run with
the largest share of them. In a Pico guest of RSP that analyses the
codes as it reads them, the 2^23 instructions of a block with the most
comparisons and branches have 4.2 M to 4.6 M of them on 12 of 13
mainnet blocks, and all of those stretches are the analysis.

riscv64 now passes all but the last 231 bytes of a code in a loop of
its own. It takes the length of an instruction from a table, in which
JUMPDEST is the one entry to stop for, and looks for the end of the
code once in eight instructions: a branch and an eighth for an
instruction of the code, in as many instructions of the machine as
before. The scalar loop finishes the code from where it stops, as it
does after the SIMD kernels of the other targets.

On the 13 blocks the stretch with the most comparisons and branches
then has 1.4 M to 2.3 M of them, and on none of them is it the
analysis.

Instructions a block in that guest, before and after:
  13 mainnet blocks   147.226 M -> 147.181 M  (-0.03 %)
  3 Sepolia blocks    424.147 M -> 424.097 M  (-0.01 %)
  45 Sepolia blocks   526.220 M -> 526.198 M  (-0.00 %)
The guests commit the same values on all 61 blocks.

riscv64 only, and hosts built with --cfg evm2_as_on_riscv64, whose own
kernels then go unused.

Tests, for every build: the loop followed by the scalar loop against
the reference analysis, on the codes the SIMD kernels are checked
with; its table against the opcode definitions; where it stops, for
codes of every length up to 700 bytes. With the cfg the EEST fixtures
run it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
